
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">How drug court works</p>
      <h1>Supervised treatment instead of a prison sentence.</h1>
      <p>What the program asks of people, and why it works better than incarceration.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="narrow">
        <h2>The basic idea</h2>
        <p>
          A person facing certain non-violent charges, whose offense is driven by a substance
          use disorder, may be referred to drug court instead of moving through traditional
          case processing. Rather than a cell, they get a treatment plan and a judge who
          watches their progress closely.
        </p>
        <p>
          The commitment is real: <strong>a minimum of one year</strong>, and most people find
          it harder than the sentence they would otherwise have served. That is the point.
          Drug courts keep people in treatment long enough for treatment to actually work.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="steps mt-6">
        <li>
          <h3>Intensive treatment and support</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Participants receive the treatment and services they need to reach and
          sustain recovery — clinical care, counseling, and the practical supports around it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Accountability to the judge</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Participants are held accountable by the drug court judge for meeting
          their obligations to the court, to the community, to their families, and to themselves.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Regular, random drug testing</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Testing is frequent and unannounced. It is what makes the
          accountability meaningful rather than theoretical.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Frequent court appearances</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Participants appear in court often so the judge can review progress
          directly, in person, rather than through paperwork.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Incentives and sanctions</h3>
          <p class="mb-0">Doing well is rewarded. Falling short brings a graduated sanction. The
          Foundation funds many of the incentives — the small, concrete recognitions that make
          progress visible.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The evidence</p>
        <h2>Why courts and county commissions keep funding this.</h2>
      </div>
      <ul class="list-check">
        <li>Drug courts reduce substance use.</li>
        <li>Drug courts reduce crime.</li>
        <li>Drug courts save public money.</li>
        <li>Drug courts restore lives.</li>
        <li>Drug courts keep families together and reunite children with parents.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="narrow">
        Drug courts currently reach only a fraction of the people in the justice system living
        with a substance use disorder. Breaking the cycle of substance use and crime means
        putting a drug court within reach of everyone who needs one — including here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The taxpayer arithmetic</p>
        <h2>$7,840 a year, or $34,898 a year.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="cost-compare">
        <div class="cost-card cost-card--prison">
          <p class="cost-card__label">Incarceration — Florida</p>
          <p class="cost-card__figure">$34,898</p>
          <span class="cost-card__unit">per person, per year</span>
          <div class="cost-bar" role="img" aria-label="Relative cost bar, shown at full width for comparison.">
            <div class="cost-bar__fill" style="width:100%"></div>
          </div>
          <p class="cost-card__desc">$95.61 per person per day — Florida Department of
          Corrections full operating cost, FY 2024&ndash;25 actual.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cost-card cost-card--court">
          <p class="cost-card__label">Drug court — Florida</p>
          <p class="cost-card__figure">$7,840</p>
          <span class="cost-card__unit">per participant, per year</span>
          <div class="cost-bar" role="img" aria-label="Relative cost bar, shown at about one fifth the width of the incarceration bar.">
            <div class="cost-bar__fill" style="width:22.5%"></div>
          </div>
          <p class="cost-card__desc">Statewide average annual cost per drug court participant,
          per the Florida Legislature's program evaluation office.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="source-note">
        <p><strong>Sources, with the caveat stated plainly.</strong></p>
        <p>
          Incarceration: Florida Office of Economic and Demographic Research, capital and
          operating costs adopted by the Criminal Justice Estimating Conference, February 2026
          (<a href="https://edr.state.fl.us/content/conferences/criminaljusticeimpact/2026DOCPerDiemandBedCosts.pdf" rel="noopener">PDF</a>).
          The $34,898 is the FY 2024&ndash;25 actual and excludes debt service and indirect
          administrative costs, so it is if anything an understatement.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0">
          Drug court: <a href="https://oppaga.fl.gov/Documents/Reports/14-02.pdf" rel="noopener">OPPAGA Report 14-02</a> (2014),
          the most recent Florida-specific per-participant figure available. It is in 2014
          dollars; inflation-adjusted it would be roughly $10,000&ndash;$11,000 today. Even on
          that basis, drug court costs well under a third of a prison bed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--tint">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero__grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">For employers</p>
          <h2>Hiring someone in the program is a better bet than you think.</h2>
          <p>
            Ask yourself how often you drug test the people already on your payroll. For most
            employers the honest answer is never.
          </p>
          <p>
            A drug court participant is tested <strong>two to three times a week</strong>, at
            random. On top of that they have a judge, a probation officer, a state attorney and a
            full treatment team all watching whether they show up and do the work. You will not
            find a more closely supervised employee anywhere.
          </p>
          <p class="mb-0">
            We work with local employers to place participants and graduates, and we are happy to
            walk you through how it works before you commit to anything.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row mt-5">
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="contact.html?subject=general">Talk to us about hiring</a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>What the Foundation adds</h3>
            <p>
              The court cannot buy a car part or a bus fare, and it cannot reach outside its own
              jurisdiction. We can. A judge who can see that somebody needs a scooter to get to
              treatment can refer that person to us and it gets handled — no budget cycle, no
              hearing.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-0">
              We charge the court nothing for any of it. It is all fundraising and donations, and
              it is one of the reasons our judges say they would not want to run this program
              without a foundation behind it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section__head">
        <h2>Common questions</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Who is eligible for drug court in Flagler County?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body">
            <div class="placeholder">
              <p class="mb-0"><strong>[CLIENT / COURT TO CONFIRM]</strong> Eligibility is set by
              the Seventh Judicial Circuit, not by the Foundation. We need the current criteria
              — charge types, prior record limits, residency, and the referral route — before
              publishing anything here, because a wrong answer could keep someone from applying.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How does someone get referred?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body">
            <div class="placeholder">
              <p class="mb-0"><strong>[CLIENT / COURT TO CONFIRM]</strong> Who initiates a
              referral — defense counsel, the State Attorney, the judge, or self-referral — and
              the contact for the drug court coordinator.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does the program take?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body">
            <p class="mb-0">A minimum of one year. The actual length depends on the person's
            progress through the program's phases.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What does the Foundation pay for?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body">
            <p class="mb-0">
              We fund the things the court budget does not cover: transportation to treatment and
              court, car repairs, driver’s license reinstatement, GEDs, housing deposits,
              specialized treatment, life skills programming, graduations, free Narcan, and
              community training. We do <em>not</em> pay for drug testing — that is part of the
              court program — and we do not pay anyone’s fines. See
              <a href="ways-to-give.html">Ways to Give</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is the Foundation part of the court?</summary>
          <div class="faq__body">
            <p class="mb-0">
              No. We are an independent 501(c)(3) nonprofit (EIN 27-1349987) that supports the
              program. Having a nonprofit backing a public agency is unusual — it is what lets
              the program act quickly without waiting on a budget cycle.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

